      {
        "slide": 3,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "An oblique view of a pecan plate lying on a long dark council table, with 39 round wells. Counted off the image, they hold 19 polished brass slotted weights (rows of 7, 6 and 6), then 15 blued (1, 6, 7 and 1), then 1 cool nickel, then 4 empty dark wells in reading order. Each weight has a boss, two engraved rings, a fine knurled edge and a slot running from the boss to the rim, all aligned toward the lower right.",
        "visual_consequence": "With the type covered, the brass block is visibly larger than the blued block, the single pale nickel weight stands apart, and the four empty wells at the end read as missing weights, the reserve. The count matches figures.json exactly: 19, 15, 1 and 4. All three metals and the holes separate at feed size.",
        "weakness": "None blocking. The taller type stays clear of the case. The hook 'The whole ceiling, in weights' sets on one line. The four-line dek and the two-line legend sit on the dark table field. The legend's second row ('MICROSOFT', 'RESERVE, EMPTY') ends about 175 native px above the plate's back edge at the left (r2c1, legend baseline near y 90, plate edge near y 265), and the gap is wider at the right (r2c2). No glyph touches a weight or the plate. The legend rides over faint pale grain filaments in the table and stays legible. The one typographic blemish: the dek breaks 'Drawn / to illustrate.', leaving a short fourth line under a long second line (r1c1, r1c2). It is legible, and rewrapping is optional. The art is unchanged from my last reading. The cite and site lines are on the plate apron, and the table grain is still stretched streak noise.",
